extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Loading indicators"

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Loading indicators
				p.lead The one piece of design the library ships, and how to replace it.


	+section("The exception to unstyled")

		p Panel and PanelSet ship #[a(href=url('/how-it-works.html#unstyled')) no styling], with one exception: the loading indicator. While async/remote/slow content loads, you get a spinner, and it has a size, a thickness, a speed and colors.

		p The spinner is there because the library needs it. Panel and PanelSet need to show #[em at least something] to indicate to the user that there is some waiting to be expected. That "at least something" is the spinner. You do not have to use it, but you do have to show something. NOT showing anything if there is async/remote/slow content loading in the background is worse.

		p You will probably want one of three things: 
		ul
			li The default spinner, in the panel area. You get it out of the box.
			li Your own spinner, with your own size, thickness, speed and colors. You can do that with CSS variables.
			li A spinner in the trigger, or a progress bar, or skeleton content, or a label change. You can do that with the state the library exposes.


		.callout
			+warning()
			p The library supplies the #[strong state], not the look. #[code .is-loading] on the panel, #[code aria-busy] and #[code .is-trigger-loading] on the trigger. The spinner is only the default way of showing that state. Replace it and nothing else changes.


	+section("Customizing: CSS variables")

		p Set them anywhere they inherit from: on #[code :root] for all of them, or on one panel to change just that one.

		h3 Appearance

		.card.api
			dl
				dt.code --ps-spinner-size
				dd How big the circle is. Default #[code 40px].

				dt.code --ps-spinner-thickness
				dd How thick the ring is. Default #[code 4px].

				dt.code --ps-spinner-track-color
				dd The ring that stays put. Default #[code rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)].

				dt.code --ps-spinner-color
				dd The arc that travels, which is what reads as movement. Default #[code rgba(0, 100, 255, 0.8)].

				dt.code --ps-spinner-speed
				dd One full turn. Default #[code 0.8s].

		+codeblock('CSS').
			:root {
				--ps-spinner-size: 24px;
				--ps-spinner-thickness: 2px;
				--ps-spinner-track-color: rgb(0 0 0 / 0.08);
				--ps-spinner-color: rebeccapurple;
				--ps-spinner-speed: 0.6s;
			}

		h3 Position

		p By default the spinner sits dead centre of the panel. These four place it, and #[code --ps-spinner-transform] is what centres it on that point.

		.card.api
			dl
				dt.code --ps-spinner-top / --ps-spinner-bottom
				dd Defaults: #[code 50%] and #[code auto].

				dt.code --ps-spinner-left / --ps-spinner-right
				dd Defaults: #[code 50%] and #[code auto].

				dt.code --ps-spinner-transform
				dd Default #[code translate(-50%, -50%)], which pulls the circle back by half its own size so that #[code 50%/50%] is its middle rather than its top-left corner. To place it against an edge instead, set this to #[code translate(0, 0)]. It has to stay a transform function: #[code none] is not valid here, because the spin is composed on top of it.

		p Putting it in the top right corner, out of the way of the content:

		+codeblock('CSS').
			#my-panel {
				--ps-spinner-top: 12px;
				--ps-spinner-right: 12px;
				--ps-spinner-left: auto;
				--ps-spinner-transform: translate(0, 0);
			}

		h3 Dimming

		.card.api
			dl
				dt.code --ps-loading-panel-opacity
				dd How far the old content fades back while the new content loads. Default #[code 0.1]. Set it to #[code 1] to leave the content alone.

				dt.code --ps-loading-dim-duration / --ps-loading-fadeout-duration
				dd Worked out from the panel speed and the opacity above. Override them directly if you change those.


	+section("Using a custom indicator")

		p Set #[code customIndicator] if you want to inform the user of the loading state yourself. If you set it, the library does not show its spinner. It is the same option and the same attribute on both:

		+codeblock('JavaScript').
			new Panel('#my-panel', { customIndicator: true });
			new PanelSet('#my-set', { customIndicator: true });

		+codeblock('HTML').
			<div data-panel data-panel-custom-indicator> … </div>
			<div data-panelset data-panel-custom-indicator> … </div>


		.callout
			+warning()
			p Taking the spinner out does not touch the #[strong state]. #[code .is-loading] and #[code aria-busy] are set either way, so show something with them, or the wait says nothing. That is what the hooks below are for.


	+section("Your own indicator")

		p Both components mark the state while content loads, and clear it the moment the load settles, whether it finishes, is cancelled, or fails. Style whatever you like off these:

		.card.api
			dl
				dt.code .is-loading
				dd On the panel or the panelset, while its content loads.

				dt.code aria-busy="true"
				dd On every trigger pointing at that panel. This is the real accessibility signal for a control whose content is being fetched, so it is worth keying off rather than the class.

				dt.code .is-trigger-loading
				dd On those same triggers, for when you want a class instead.

		p The default spinner and whatever you add are independent, so it is worth deciding which one you want. Two indicators for one wait is noise, so if you put something in the trigger, hide the panel one.

		p A spinner in the trigger rather than the panel is an alternative that can easily be constructed. It works the same for both: the button of a Panel, the tab of a PanelSet. Try them:

		.grid-2.gap-v-1
			div
				h3 Panel
				.demo.uses-trigger-spinner
					button(aria-controls="li-panel" aria-expanded="false") Load 'pricing'
					#li-panel(data-panel data-panel-custom-indicator data-panel-loading-height="0")
						.panel-wrapper
							// content cloned in by JS, from the template below

			div
				h3 PanelSet
				.demo.uses-trigger-spinner
					.tabs(role="tablist" aria-label="Loading indicator tabs")
						button(role="tab" aria-controls="li-tab-1" aria-selected="true") Features
						button(role="tab" aria-controls="li-tab-2" aria-selected="false") Load 'pricing'
					#li-tabs.tab-content(data-panelset data-panel-custom-indicator data-loading-height="0" data-loading-delay="150")
						.panel-wrapper
							#li-tab-1(role="tabpanel" class="active" data-loaded="true")
								+example-1(4)
							#li-tab-2(role="tabpanel" hidden)
								// content cloned in by JS, from the template below

		//- What the two demos load.
		template#li-panel-content
			+example-2(4)
		template#li-tab-content
			+example-2(4)

		p Both of those work the same way: say that you are showing it yourself, then show it on the trigger.

		+codeblock('HTML').
			<div data-panel data-panel-custom-indicator data-panel-loading-height="0"> … </div>

		+codeblock('CSS').
			/* show it on the trigger: a button, or a tab */
			[aria-busy="true"]::after {
				content: "";
				display: inline-block;
				width: 14px;
				height: 14px;
				margin-left: 0.5em;
				border: 2px solid rgb(0 0 0 / 0.15);
				border-top-color: currentColor;
				border-radius: 50%;
				animation: my-spin 0.6s linear infinite;
			}

			@keyframes my-spin {
				to { rotate: 360deg; }
			}

		p #[code loadingHeight: 0] is the other half of it: with the indicator on the trigger, the content area does not need to hold space for one, so it stays collapsed until the real content arrives. That is why the demos above open straight to their content.

		p The state is just a state: fade the trigger, put a progress bar above the panel, show skeleton content, swap the label. The library does not care what you show.


append scripts
	if isProd
		script(src=`${basePath}lib/panelset.js` vite-ignore)
		script.
			Panel.init('.docs-content [data-panel]');
			PanelSet.init();
		script(src=`${basePath}assets/scripts/example-loading.js` vite-ignore)
	else
		script(type="module").
			import { Panel, PanelSet } from 'panelset';
			Panel.init('.docs-content [data-panel]');
			PanelSet.init();
		script(type="module" src="/src/assets/scripts/example-loading.js")
